window.SM_EVOLUTION = [{"date":"2024-03","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2024-03 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2024/mar/","sourcePdfPages":[4,6],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Page breaks and bullet glyphs normalised; publication metadata excluded.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"The outlook for the global economy has improved following the peak in inflation, but global financial stability risks remain elevated.","score":0.26166319811948424,"words":[{"text":"improved","start":39,"end":47,"base":2.1,"score":2.1,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"Financial market participants have been increasingly optimistic about the prospects for a soft landing in the global economy.","score":0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"optimistic","start":53,"end":63,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A substantial easing cycle in monetary policy is expected over the next two years or so, with inflation returning to central banks’ targets and unemployment rising only modestly.","score":-0.6597279468503828,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":14,"end":20,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"unemployment","start":144,"end":156,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Although pressures from high inflation and tight monetary policy continue to weigh on many households and businesses, a number of developments – including the resolution of supply chain disruptions, declines in energy prices, continued strength in labour markets, strong household balance sheets and solid corporate earnings – have contributed to the global economy’s resilience to date.","score":0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"disruptions","start":186,"end":197,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"declines","start":199,"end":207,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"strength","start":236,"end":244,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"strong","start":264,"end":270,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The capital position of large international banks leaves them well placed to weather a decline in asset quality and/or worsening macroeconomic conditions.","score":-0.6704783996548059,"words":[{"text":"decline","start":87,"end":94,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"worsening","start":119,"end":128,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, several economies, including the United States, have a sizeable tail of smaller banks, some of which are more vulnerable due to asset quality and profitability concerns.","score":-0.44970406437185423,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":119,"end":129,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"profitability","start":155,"end":168,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"concerns","start":169,"end":177,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Although risks to the outlook for the global economy have become more balanced as inflation has eased, risks to global financial stability remain.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"These risks have the potential to spill over to the Australian financial system, via their impact on the local economy through trade channels and/or an increase in risk aversion that results in tighter global financial conditions.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This would add to the financial pressures experienced by domestic borrowers and, to the extent this puts significant strain on financial institutions’ balance sheets, limit access to credit in the Australian economy.","score":-0.051571062312939675,"words":[{"text":"strain","start":117,"end":123,"base":-0.2,"score":-0.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Among the global risks, several stand out as having the potential to adversely affect financial stability in Australia: • Further weakness in the Chinese property sector could interact with longstanding macro-financial vulnerabilities there.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"adversely","start":69,"end":78,"base":-0.8,"score":-0.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"weakness","start":130,"end":138,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":219,"end":234,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If stresses in the Chinese economy and financial system intensified or broadened, they could spill over to the rest of the world (including Australia) through trade channels and an increase in global risk aversion.","score":-0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"stresses","start":3,"end":11,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Conditions in global commercial real estate (CRE) markets could deteriorate further.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"deteriorate","start":66,"end":77,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"High interest rates and ongoing weak demand, particularly for older or lower quality offices, continue to put pressure on borrowers.","score":-0.44043357076016854,"words":[{"text":"weak","start":32,"end":36,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While the direct risks are largest in those international banking systems highly exposed to CRE lending, conditions in Australian CRE markets could be affected by the repatriation of foreign investment, following a decade or so where foreign investment in Australian CRE increased notably.","score":-0.10019088044825505,"words":[{"text":"exposed","start":81,"end":88,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.39,"notes":[{"t":"boost","w":"highly","m":1.3}],"note":"intensified highly ×1.30"}]},{"text":"• Worse-than-expected macroeconomic outcomes, coupled with fragilities in market functioning, could result in a disorderly adjustment in financial asset prices.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"Worse","start":2,"end":7,"base":-2.1,"score":-2.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"disorderly","start":112,"end":122,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Market participants’ expectations for a soft landing in the global economy, as seen in low risk premia and volatility, could leave financial markets vulnerable to an adverse shock.","score":-0.709560194985489,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":107,"end":117,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerable","start":149,"end":159,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":166,"end":173,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Possible triggers for an abrupt repricing in market risk premia include global inflation proving more persistent than expected or a severe adverse geopolitical event.","score":-0.8442148522271654,"words":[{"text":"abrupt","start":25,"end":31,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"persistent","start":102,"end":112,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":132,"end":138,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":139,"end":146,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Disruptions to the functioning of global financial markets could be amplified by shortcomings in the management of leverage and liquidity mismatches, including by non-bank financial intermediaries (NBFIs) in key financial centres as seen on several occasions in recent years.","score":-0.5993731596731062,"words":[{"text":"Disruptions","start":0,"end":11,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"mismatches","start":138,"end":148,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Threats originating outside the global and domestic financial system continue to build.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"Threats","start":2,"end":9,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These include cyber-attacks, risks associated with climate change and geopolitical tensions.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Most Australian households and businesses remain able to service their debt and meet essential expenses.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"able","start":49,"end":53,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Conditions will remain challenging for many households and businesses in Australia this year.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":23,"end":34,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This is especially true for lower income households, including many renters, and the indebted households already facing acute budget pressures.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Under the economic outlook presented in the February Statement on Monetary Policy, these pressures are expected to gradually ease over the next few years as inflation declines and real incomes rise.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"declines","start":167,"end":175,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, most households have continued to service their debt.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Strong conditions in the labour market, the large savings buffers accumulated by many borrowers during the pandemic and rising housing prices are helping households to adapt to challenging economic conditions.","score":0.2022886949696694,"words":[{"text":"Strong","start":0,"end":6,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenging","start":177,"end":188,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Many households have made adjustments, including reducing their discretionary spending and saving, increasing their hours of work, and some have drawn down on saving buffers.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The strong financial starting position of many businesses should help to limit risks to financial stability.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":4,"end":10,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While the profit margins of many businesses are around pre-pandemic levels, some businesses are likely to remain under financial pressure over the coming period as sales growth remains subdued; in addition, strong growth in the costs of labour and other inputs is expected to moderate only gradually.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":207,"end":213,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This is particularly true of discretionary sectors, as many households have pulled back on non-essential consumption, and conditions remain challenging in parts of the construction sector.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":140,"end":151,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Yet, the overall level of profitability and strong balance sheets among businesses reduce the risk of widespread financial stress, and arrears on bank loans to businesses are low.","score":0.025811286645983332,"words":[{"text":"profitability","start":26,"end":39,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"strong","start":44,"end":50,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":123,"end":129,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"arrears","start":135,"end":142,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Conditions remain challenging in domestic CRE (in particular, office) markets, though there is little evidence to date of financial stress among owners of Australian CRE.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":18,"end":29,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":132,"end":138,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Prices continue to ease and transaction volumes remain subdued, though there have been few signs of distressed sales.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"distressed","start":100,"end":110,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"One key risk to conditions in the domestic CRE market stems from the possible withdrawal of foreign lenders and investors owing to a further deterioration in international CRE markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"deterioration","start":141,"end":154,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Some domestic NBFIs also have material exposures to CRE, though some (including superannuation funds and unit trusts) use little or no leverage and most have measures in place to manage the effects of investor redemptions.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The limited size and more conservative nature of bank lending to CRE in Australia than in the past means the systemic risks to the banking system are lower than in previous downturns in CRE markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"downturns","start":173,"end":182,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Overall, the risks to the financial system from lending to households and businesses remain contained based on the current economic outlook.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"While banks expect loan arrears to pick up further, most Australian households and businesses appear positioned to manage the pressure on their finances from inflation and interest rates.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"arrears","start":24,"end":31,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, the expected easing in labour market conditions and subdued growth in activity are likely to present further challenges for some households and businesses this year.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":22,"end":28,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"challenges","start":118,"end":128,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"These challenges would intensify if economic conditions were to deteriorate by more than expected or if inflation is more persistent than forecast in the February Statement.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":6,"end":16,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"deteriorate","start":64,"end":75,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"persistent","start":122,"end":132,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system has a high level of resilience and is well positioned to continue to support the economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Australian banks are well prepared to handle an expected increase in loan losses in the period ahead.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":74,"end":80,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This expected increase in loan losses would be coming from a very low base.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":31,"end":37,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks’ overall capital levels are high, profits are healthy and the very low percentage of borrowers in negative equity on their loans further protects banks against credit losses.","score":-0.8359758677591163,"words":[{"text":"negative","start":104,"end":112,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"against","start":158,"end":165,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":173,"end":179,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The recent stress test conducted by the Australian Prudential Regulation Authority concluded that large banks could continue to make credit available to borrowers even in a severe (but plausible) economic downturn.","score":-0.7140335895433495,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":11,"end":17,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"severe","start":173,"end":179,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"downturn","start":205,"end":213,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Banks also have strong liquidity positions, which should assist them if there were to be temporary funding market disruptions.","score":0.22634795467253555,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":16,"end":22,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptions","start":114,"end":125,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Risks to financial stability posed by Australian NBFIs are relatively contained.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Around half of the Australian NBFI sector is comprised of prudentially regulated superannuation funds, which have low leverage and the ability to pass on investment risks to their members (in contrast to the greater risk profile of NBFIs in global financial centres).","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"greater","start":208,"end":215,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While the overall risks in the Australian NBFI sector appear to be contained, data gaps (including in relation to CRE activities) prevent the identification of potential vulnerabilities in less-regulated NBFIs.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":170,"end":185,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As a result, member agencies of the Council of Financial Regulators continue to pursue work aimed at strengthening the visibility and monitoring of NBFIs’ activities in Australia.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":101,"end":114,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening institutions’ operational resilience to threats emanating from outside the financial system remains a regulatory priority.","score":0.10273309938750286,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"threats","start":54,"end":61,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Financial institutions’ operational resilience is critical to the overall resilience of the Australian financial system.","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":50,"end":58,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The adoption of new technologies, including artificial intelligence and cloud infrastructures, brings many benefits but also risks, particularly in an environment of escalating cyber threats, geopolitical risks and increasing use of third-party providers.","score":-0.7875767884122276,"words":[{"text":"escalating","start":166,"end":176,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"threats","start":183,"end":190,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Strong governance and operational risk management practices by financial institutions are critical to maintaining operational resilience in a high-threat environment.","score":-0.3399500518250425,"words":[{"text":"Strong","start":0,"end":6,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"critical","start":90,"end":98,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"threat","start":147,"end":153,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This will continue to be a key area of focus for financial regulators in Australia.","score":0,"words":[]}]},{"date":"2024-09","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2024-09 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2024/sep/","sourcePdfPages":[4,7],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Page breaks and bullet glyphs normalised; publication metadata excluded.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"While inflation has eased, the global economic outlook continues to be uncertain and vulnerabilities in the global financial system remain.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":85,"end":100,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The finances of many households and businesses in advanced economies continue to be resilient, despite ongoing pressure from tight monetary policy and inflation.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"despite","start":95,"end":102,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This resilience has been supported by firm, albeit softening, conditions in labour markets, a stabilisation or pick-up in real household incomes, and solid corporate earnings.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"While there is a small but growing group of borrowers experiencing financial stress in these economies, a further easing in inflation − and with it, lower policy rates − is expected to support the balance sheets and cash flows of households and firms over the period ahead.","score":-0.7875767884122276,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":77,"end":83,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"easing","start":114,"end":120,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The central expectation for many countries, including Australia, remains a modest economic cycle, but this outcome is by no means assured.","score":-0.3291459381423852,"words":[{"text":"assured","start":130,"end":137,"base":1.5,"score":-0.8999999999999999,"notes":[{"t":"neg","w":"no"},{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"negated no · after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Considerable uncertainty about the outlook remains, and there have been bouts of market volatility over recent months.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":88,"end":98,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A significant economic downturn, including a sharp deterioration in labour markets, is the principal risk to the resilience of borrowers.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":23,"end":31,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"deterioration","start":51,"end":64,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The sizeable capital buffers maintained by large banks worldwide position them well to handle rising loan impairments in such a scenario and continue supporting the economy.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"impairments","start":106,"end":117,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, threats originating from outside the financial system – including geopolitical risks and risks associated with climate change – also continue to increase and have the potential to adversely interact with vulnerabilities in the global financial system.","score":-0.7782533221506259,"words":[{"text":"threats","start":9,"end":16,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"adversely","start":189,"end":198,"base":-0.8,"score":-0.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":213,"end":228,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Three vulnerabilities stand out as having the potential to significantly impact financial stability in Australia: • Operational vulnerabilities resulting from increased complexity and interconnectedness in the digital economy.","score":-0.295958174200194,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":6,"end":21,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":128,"end":143,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Digitalisation and rapid technological development are transforming how the economy and financial system operate.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This is delivering speed and efficiency gains, lowering costs and improving the consumer experience.","score":0.7717372111011058,"words":[{"text":"efficiency","start":29,"end":39,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"gains","start":40,"end":45,"base":1.4,"score":1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"improving","start":66,"end":75,"base":1.8,"score":1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"But it also comes with an increase in complexity and interconnectedness.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Technological innovations – such as artificial intelligence and cloud computing – have led to increasing concentration risk in third-party providers and raised the risks of central points of failure in the financial system.","score":-0.2022886949696694,"words":[{"text":"innovations","start":14,"end":25,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"failure","start":191,"end":198,"base":-2.3,"score":-2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Recent incidents have highlighted the vulnerability of the economy and financial system to technological outages and underscored the need to strengthen operational resilience within firms and across their networks.","score":-0.5573704017131537,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":7,"end":16,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerability","start":38,"end":51,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"outages","start":105,"end":112,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"strengthen","start":141,"end":151,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Advancing digitalisation is occurring at a time of heightened geopolitical tensions, which increases the prospect of cyber-attacks that could have systemic implications.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Advancing","start":0,"end":9,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Low risk premia and leveraged positions increase the potential for a disorderly adjustment in global asset prices in response to negative news.","score":-0.7351470441147052,"words":[{"text":"disorderly","start":71,"end":81,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"negative","start":131,"end":139,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Low risk premia in a number of major asset classes, particularly equities and credit, makes global asset prices sensitive to negative surprises.","score":-0.5718850320700721,"words":[{"text":"negative","start":125,"end":133,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could set off disorderly price adjustments and disrupt the funding markets that Australian businesses and financial institutions use extensively.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"disorderly","start":19,"end":29,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disrupt","start":52,"end":59,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The bout of heightened global market volatility in early August highlighted the risk that disappointing economic or earnings news, or worsening geopolitical tensions, could trigger such an event.","score":-0.8271299960237043,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":37,"end":47,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disappointing","start":90,"end":103,"base":-2.2,"score":-2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"worsening","start":134,"end":143,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Further increases in government debt in key advanced economies could also make these markets more sensitive to adverse shocks, including those that exacerbate concerns about debt sustainability.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":111,"end":118,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"exacerbate","start":148,"end":158,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"concerns","start":159,"end":167,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As recent years have shown, the leverage and interlinkages of non-bank financial intermediaries with banks could also amplify the effects of shocks to the global financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• Imbalances in China’s financial sector.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Imbalances","start":2,"end":12,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Longstanding vulnerabilities in part of the Chinese financial system – including banks, non-banks and local governments – have been exacerbated by the ongoing weakness in the Chinese real estate sector.","score":-0.7095601949854891,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":13,"end":28,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"exacerbated","start":132,"end":143,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"weakness","start":159,"end":167,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A further loss of confidence – absent a timely and significant response from the Chinese authorities – could see stress spill over to the rest of the Chinese economy and financial system, which would likely affect the global economy and financial system.","score":-0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"loss","start":10,"end":14,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":113,"end":119,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Should these risks and vulnerabilities materialise, spillovers to the Australian financial system could occur in the following ways: • Directly and rapidly through a severe operational disruption – including to national infrastructure or to a key financial institution.","score":-0.6907747429922022,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":23,"end":38,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":166,"end":172,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruption","start":185,"end":195,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Via a significant increase in risk aversion in global financial markets – to the extent that it sharply raises costs and limits Australian firms’ and financial institutions’ access to funding and liquidity in global markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"sharply","start":98,"end":105,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This would exacerbate financial pressures on domestic borrowers and, to the extent this puts significant strain on financial institutions’ balance sheets, limit access to credit in the Australian economy.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"exacerbate","start":11,"end":21,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"strain","start":105,"end":111,"base":-0.2,"score":-0.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, the exchange rate would also depreciate, providing an economic and financial stabilising mechanism.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• Via the impact on the real economy – through trade and investment channels, particularly in the case of a sharp downturn in China.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":114,"end":122,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Risks to the Australian financial system from lending to households, businesses and commercial real estate (CRE) remain contained.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Budget pressures from high inflation and restrictive monetary policy continue to be felt across the Australian community, but the share of borrowers experiencing severe financial stress remains small.","score":-0.7963890709347511,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":162,"end":168,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"stress","start":179,"end":185,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"While a small but rising share of Australian households are falling behind on their mortgage repayments, the vast majority of borrowers continue to be able to service their debts and most have maintained, if not added, to their mortgage buffers.","score":0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"able","start":151,"end":155,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Many businesses also continue to manage pressure on their cash flows and balance sheets, supported by their strong financial positions prior to the rise of inflation and interest rates.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":108,"end":114,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Nevertheless, business conditions remain challenging for many firms, and small businesses in particular.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":41,"end":52,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Business insolvencies have increased sharply over the past couple of years following the removal of pandemic-era support, though they are only slightly above pre-pandemic levels as a share of all businesses.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"insolvencies","start":9,"end":21,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"sharply","start":37,"end":44,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Financial pressures are expected to ease in the period ahead, but the economic outlook is highly uncertain.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Based on the forecasts presented in the August Statement on Monetary Policy, budget pressures are expected to ease as inflation moderates further and Stage 3 tax cuts take effect.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, the expected easing in labour market conditions and subdued growth in activity will be challenging for some households and businesses.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":22,"end":28,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"challenging","start":96,"end":107,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Stress on households and businesses would be magnified if economic conditions deteriorated further than anticipated and/or if inflation and interest rates were to remain high for longer than expected.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"Stress","start":0,"end":6,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"deteriorated","start":78,"end":90,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The risk of widespread financial stress remains limited due to the generally strong financial positions of most borrowers.","score":0.12803687993289592,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":33,"end":39,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"strong","start":77,"end":83,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Very few mortgage borrowers are in negative equity, limiting the impact on lenders in the event of default and supporting their ability to continue providing credit to the economy.","score":-0.7351470441147052,"words":[{"text":"negative","start":35,"end":43,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"default","start":99,"end":106,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Most businesses that have entered insolvency are small and have little debt, limiting the broader impact on the labour market and thus household incomes, and on the capital position of lenders.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"insolvency","start":34,"end":44,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Domestic vulnerabilities could increase if households respond to any easing in financial conditions by taking on excessive debt.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":9,"end":24,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"easing","start":69,"end":75,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"excessive","start":113,"end":122,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Historically, periods of low and/or falling interest rates have coincided with borrowers taking on higher levels of debt and, in some cases, lenders extending credit to riskier borrowers.","score":-0.33995005182504245,"words":[{"text":"riskier","start":169,"end":176,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could be magnified if lending standards drop.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"International experience has highlighted the danger of boom-bust asset price cycles, particularly those amplified by the widespread use of borrowed money.","score":-0.22634795467253555,"words":[{"text":"danger","start":45,"end":51,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"boom","start":55,"end":59,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Residential property stands out in this regard.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Conditions in segments of international and domestic CRE markets remain challenging, particularly in secondary grade office buildings, but the financial stability risks in Australia remain contained.","score":-0.1901172751573434,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":72,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"Despite large declines in asset valuations over the past couple of years, overall indicators of financial stress in the Australian CRE market are low by historical standards.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"Despite","start":0,"end":7,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"declines","start":14,"end":22,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":106,"end":112,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"One risk scenario is that stress in overseas CRE markets spills over to Australian market conditions via interconnected sources of ownership and funding.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":26,"end":32,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While this could lead to losses for some investors and non-bank lenders, it is unlikely to materially affect the asset quality of domestic banks given their relatively limited CRE exposures and conservative lending standards to the sector.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":25,"end":31,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system continues to display a high level of resilience.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Australian banks have maintained prudent lending standards and are well positioned to continue supplying credit to the economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A deterioration in economic conditions or temporary disruption to funding markets is unlikely to halt lending activity.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"deterioration","start":2,"end":15,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruption","start":52,"end":62,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"halt","start":97,"end":101,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks have anticipated an increase in loan arrears and have capital and liquidity buffers well above regulatory requirements.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"arrears","start":43,"end":50,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Arrears in Australian non-bank lenders’ loan books have picked up, but system-wide risks to financial stability remain contained.","score":-0.1901172751573434,"words":[{"text":"Arrears","start":0,"end":7,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"The sector has continued to expand, including by taking market share from banks in business lending.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, systemic risks from the sector remain limited due to the sector’s small size and that its core funding is not sourced from banks.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"That said, detailed analysis of underlying credit quality is challenging due to limited data availability.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":61,"end":72,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The significant growth of the superannuation sector and its connections to Australian banks has increased its importance to financial system stability.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"stability","start":141,"end":150,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The sector has historically posed little risk to the financial system owing to its smaller footprint in funding Australian banks and corporations, limited use of leverage, and steady inflows of defined contributions that simply pass-through (rather than guarantee) returns to members.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, the sector’s rapid growth (now making up one-quarter of the financial system), the rise in herding around common benchmarks and increased exposure to margin calls (including from the hedging of foreign asset exposures) mean the sector’s investment decisions and liquidity risk management practices have a greater potential than before to amplify shocks in the financial system.","score":0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"greater","start":314,"end":321,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"For this reason, APRA is stepping up the intensity of its prudential supervision of superannuation funds.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Lifting and maintaining operational resilience in an increasingly digitalised and interconnected financial system will require a sustained and proactive effort.","score":0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"proactive","start":143,"end":152,"base":1.8,"score":1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The operational resilience of financial institutions and infrastructures is crucial for the stability of the Australian financial system.","score":0,"words":[{"text":"crucial","start":76,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stability","start":92,"end":101,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Digitalisation brings many benefits, but also new and more complex operational risks and vulnerabilities.","score":-0.22634795467253555,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":89,"end":104,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"These could interact with (and amplify) other risks, including geopolitical risk, with potentially severe consequences.","score":-0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":99,"end":105,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening operational resilience remains a regulatory priority in Australia and globally.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strong governance and operational risk management practices by financial institutions is essential in today’s high-threat environment.","score":-0.02581128664598339,"words":[{"text":"Strong","start":0,"end":6,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"threat","start":115,"end":121,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This requires an ongoing effort by industry, and regulators in Australia and internationally are stepping up the intensity of their demands in response.","score":0,"words":[]}]},{"date":"2025-04","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2025-04 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2025/apr/","sourcePdfPages":[4,8],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Original heading ellipses retained; standalone punctuation and endnotes excluded from counts.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"The Australian financial system has continued to display a high level of resilience.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Risks to the Australian financial system from lending to households, businesses and commercial real estate have remained contained.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Budget pressures remain pervasive across the Australian community, but they have eased a little for some and the share of borrowers experiencing severe financial stress remains small, reflecting the continued strength in the labour market and the maintenance of prudent lending standards.","score":-0.549915172454543,"words":[{"text":"pervasive","start":24,"end":33,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"severe","start":145,"end":151,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"stress","start":162,"end":168,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"strength","start":209,"end":217,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"After earlier increases, the share of households that have fallen behind on their mortgages appears to have stabilised at pre-pandemic levels and almost all borrowers now benefit from home values that exceed their mortgage balances (substantially so in many cases).","score":0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"benefit","start":171,"end":178,"base":2,"score":2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Company insolvencies have picked up over the past couple of years to be at the top of the range observed in the 2010s – particularly among smaller firms that face a challenging operating environment – although on a cumulative basis they remain slightly below their pre-pandemic trend.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"insolvencies","start":8,"end":20,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenging","start":165,"end":176,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Additionally, broader spillovers to the financial system have been limited, largely due to these firms’ small size and limited bank debt.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Overall, most household and business borrowers and owners of commercial real estate have been able to manage the pressures on their finances.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"able","start":94,"end":98,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This has helped maintain credit quality across the financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Australian banks’ resilience has been supported by a long period of prudent lending standards, the high quality and quantity of capital, and large liquid asset buffers.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Banks have steadily increased their capital over the past decade – largely through retained earnings – to be well above regulatory requirements.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Despite some borrowers experiencing severe financial stress, overall asset quality has remained high and loan losses have been minimal.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"Despite","start":0,"end":7,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":36,"end":42,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":53,"end":59,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":110,"end":116,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The financial stability risk posed by the non-bank financial (NBFI) sector in Australia is contained by its composition.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A small share of NBFI assets is held by entities that operate outside the regulatory perimeter and have risky features, such as high leverage or opaque business structures.","score":-0.20228869496966945,"words":[{"text":"risky","start":104,"end":109,"base":-0.8,"score":-0.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"By contrast, a relatively large share is held by APRA-regulated superannuation funds, which are primarily defined contribution funds that pass through investment risk to their members and hence do not directly bear the consequences of market outcomes.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"These funds are restricted from taking on leverage directly, and most still benefit from a steady net inflow of liquidity from members, further mitigating the risk they pose to financial stability.","score":0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"benefit","start":76,"end":83,"base":2,"score":2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, given the sector is now a large participant in key financial markets, liquidity challenges for the broader financial system could arise in the event of large shocks to the superannuation sector; for example, where an unexpected policy change allowing for early withdrawal of superannuation balances occurred alongside capital calls on private asset commitments and a large, sustained decline in the Australian dollar drained liquidity through payments related to foreign exchange hedges.","score":-0.8673649643743679,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":89,"end":99,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"unexpected","start":226,"end":236,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"decline","start":393,"end":400,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The global financial system has proved resilient to a range of shocks in recent years.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Easing inflation and lower policy rates in advanced economies have reduced the pressure on households and businesses, although stress has picked up in pockets of the corporate sector where profits are under pressure.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"Easing","start":0,"end":6,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":127,"end":133,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Robust labour markets have been key in maintaining the resilience of households, while most businesses have been supported by solid earnings and cash buffers.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, economic growth has remained sluggish, labour markets have eased in many economies, and rapid shifts in trade and fiscal policies could alter the trajectory of global growth and undo some of the progress on inflation.","score":0.038700818724480356,"words":[{"text":"sluggish","start":38,"end":46,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"progress","start":204,"end":212,"base":1.8,"score":1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"A significant economic downturn, including a sharp deterioration in labour markets, is the principal risk to the resilience of borrowers.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":23,"end":31,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"deterioration","start":51,"end":64,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Yet the sizeable capital and liquidity buffers maintained by large banks in advanced economies, including Australia, should help them to navigate a scenario where economic conditions deteriorate, while continuing to support the economy.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"deteriorate","start":183,"end":194,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Heightened geopolitical tensions and policy uncertainty in major economies has the potential to interact with existing vulnerabilities.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":119,"end":134,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Ongoing uncertainty about the United States’ international trade policies, and the reactions this may trigger, could have a chilling effect on business investment and household spending decisions, and pose substantial headwinds to the outlook for global economic activity and inflation.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"There is also considerable uncertainty about the effects of possible fiscal, regulatory and other government policy changes on global growth and inflation.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"All these uncertainties add to existing risks from cyber and operational incidents and climate change shocks.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":73,"end":82,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Three key vulnerabilities stand out as having the potential to significantly affect financial stability in Australia:1 • Vulnerabilities in key international financial markets, amplified by longstanding vulnerabilities in the global NBFI sector.","score":-0.42146361521176223,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":10,"end":25,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"Vulnerabilities","start":121,"end":136,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":203,"end":218,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Compressed risk premia and concentration of exposures in equity markets increase the likelihood that adverse news – triggered by any number of global risks in a highly uncertain environment – sparks a disorderly correction in global asset prices.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":101,"end":108,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disorderly","start":201,"end":211,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"correction","start":212,"end":222,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Rising leverage and the risk of liquidity mismatches among some NBFIs has the potential to amplify such a shock.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"mismatches","start":42,"end":52,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Imbalances in China’s financial sector.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Imbalances","start":2,"end":12,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Chinese policymakers appear to have adopted a more supportive counter-cyclical policy stance of late, but in easing financial conditions, these policies could exacerbate long-term debt vulnerabilities in the Chinese financial system.","score":-0.8461856184131908,"words":[{"text":"late","start":96,"end":100,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"easing","start":109,"end":115,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"exacerbate","start":159,"end":169,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":185,"end":200,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"US tariffs on Chinese imports may necessitate a further policy response from the Chinese authorities to support economic activity.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"If macro-financial risks were to materialise in China, stress could spill over into the global financial system, including Australia, via trade channels and increased risk aversion in global financial markets.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":55,"end":61,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Operational vulnerabilities resulting from growing complexity and interconnectedness.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":14,"end":29,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While digitalisation offers the potential for substantial efficiency gains in the financial system, it can also increase the complexity and interdependence in supporting systems.","score":0.5993731596731062,"words":[{"text":"efficiency","start":58,"end":68,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"gains","start":69,"end":74,"base":1.4,"score":1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As a result, operational systems in key financial market infrastructure and key institutions are increasingly vulnerable to technology outages and malicious cyber-attacks.","score":-0.709560194985489,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":110,"end":120,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"outages","start":135,"end":142,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"malicious","start":147,"end":156,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The threat landscape for operational risk could worsen further in the context of escalating geopolitical tensions.","score":-0.8481222655051033,"words":[{"text":"threat","start":4,"end":10,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"worsen","start":48,"end":54,"base":-2.3,"score":-2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"escalating","start":81,"end":91,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If risks were to materialise, these vulnerabilities could cause spillover effects to the Australian financial system in three main ways: • Via a significant increase in risk aversion in global financial markets.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":36,"end":51,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could sharply increase financing costs, including in Australia, and restrict Australian firms’ and financial institutions’ access to funding and liquidity in global markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"sharply","start":11,"end":18,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"It could also create liquidity strains for Australian banks and NBFIs, such as superannuation funds.","score":-0.295958174200194,"words":[{"text":"strains","start":31,"end":38,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Such an event would intensify financial pressures on domestic borrowers and, if severe enough to strain financial institutions’ balance sheets, could limit credit availability in the Australian economy.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":80,"end":86,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"strain","start":97,"end":103,"base":-0.2,"score":-0.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, there is considerable scope for most borrowers and lenders to draw down on buffers in the event of a liquidity shock, and any depreciation of the exchange rate would similarly play a shock-absorbing role for the wider economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• Via the impact on the outlook for the real economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A global economic downturn, particularly one that leads to a sharp slowdown in China (Australia’s most significant trading partner), could negatively affect Australia through trade channels – including commodity prices and investment – and spill over into weaker spending by Australian consumers and businesses.","score":-0.8555415792658776,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":18,"end":26,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"slowdown","start":67,"end":75,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"negatively","start":139,"end":149,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"weaker","start":256,"end":262,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Via a severe operational disruption.","score":-0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":8,"end":14,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruption","start":27,"end":37,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A direct and rapid impact could arise from disruptions to financial system and national infrastructure, or to a key financial institution, and could also undermine public confidence.","score":-0.5573704017131537,"words":[{"text":"disruptions","start":43,"end":54,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"undermine","start":154,"end":163,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system is well placed to continue to provide vital services in the event of a severe downturn.","score":-0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":103,"end":109,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"downturn","start":110,"end":118,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Cash flow pressures on borrowers will remain widespread in the near term but are expected to ease a little further.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The forecasts presented in the February Statement on Monetary Policy (based on the market-implied cash rate path at that time) suggested that most households and businesses would see some improvements in their cash flow positions over the months ahead, supported by an improvement in the economic environment and easing financial conditions.","score":0.42146361521176223,"words":[{"text":"improvements","start":188,"end":200,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"improvement","start":269,"end":280,"base":2,"score":2,"notes":[]},{"text":"easing","start":313,"end":319,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, the most vulnerable borrowers will continue to face significant challenges.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":18,"end":28,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"challenges","start":73,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Considerable uncertainty surrounds the outlook.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"If the economy, and thus the labour market, proves materially weaker than assumed in the central forecast or if financial conditions do not ease as much as markets expect, a larger number of borrowers would experience stress, other things equal.","score":-0.6907747429922022,"words":[{"text":"weaker","start":62,"end":68,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":218,"end":224,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Additionally, if downside risks to the global outlook materialise, they could spill over to some Australian businesses via trade linkages or tighter access to offshore funding markets.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Nevertheless, the strong financial positions of most households, businesses and owners of commercial real estate are likely to limit the risk of widespread financial stress.","score":0.19011727515734328,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":18,"end":24,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"stress","start":166,"end":172,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Even in the event of a significant economic downturn, banks are well positioned to absorb large loan losses while continuing to support the economy through lending to households and businesses.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":44,"end":52,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":101,"end":107,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks are well provisioned for loan losses and continue to maintain capital and liquidity buffers well above regulatory requirements.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":36,"end":42,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The superannuation sector has in the past generally displayed a high level of resilience and funds’ activities have tended to support financial stability.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"While the sector supports long-term capital formation in Australia and has previously been a supplier of liquidity to the system in periods of financial stress, the growth and size of the sector now introduces the potential for it to amplify stress if several extreme-but-plausible liquidity risks materialised simultaneously.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":153,"end":159,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"stress","start":242,"end":248,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"It is also exposed to the risk of operational disruptions.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"exposed","start":11,"end":18,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptions","start":46,"end":57,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Continued strengthening of superannuation funds’ governance and liquidity and operational risk management practices is therefore an area of ongoing focus of regulators.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":10,"end":23,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The general insurance sector also displays resilience, but insurance affordability and availability may become increasingly challenging over time.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":124,"end":135,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The general insurance sector is well capitalised and profitability has been supported by low claims, higher premiums and a moderation in the growth of reinsurance costs.","score":-0.1027330993875028,"words":[{"text":"profitability","start":53,"end":66,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"claims","start":93,"end":99,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, claims are expected to rise due to the impact of Cyclone Alfred in Queensland and New South Wales in March.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"claims","start":9,"end":15,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"And insured losses from the Los Angeles wildfires in January could drive up global reinsurance costs.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":12,"end":18,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could put upward pressure on home insurance premiums in Australia, further reducing affordability in areas at risk of natural perils.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"perils","start":131,"end":137,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These trends could continue as climate change intensifies weather-related risks to physical infrastructure over time.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"If this were to lead to declining insurance coverage for mortgaged properties, banks may be increasingly exposed to financial losses from physical climate risk, potentially leading to financial stability risks in the longer term.","score":-0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"declining","start":24,"end":33,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"exposed","start":105,"end":112,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":126,"end":132,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"leading","start":173,"end":180,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, it is important that lending standards remain sound …","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Looking further ahead, resilience could be undermined if lending standards deteriorate and households respond to an actual or anticipated easing in financial conditions by accumulating excessive debt.","score":-0.8401680504168059,"words":[{"text":"undermined","start":43,"end":53,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"deteriorate","start":75,"end":86,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"easing","start":138,"end":144,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"excessive","start":185,"end":194,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While lending standards have been very sound in recent years, and the ratio of net household debt to income has been little changed, the RBA and other regulators will closely monitor for signs of emerging housing-related vulnerabilities.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":221,"end":236,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In the business sector, an actual or anticipated easing in financial conditions does not appear likely to contribute to a material build-up of vulnerabilities given the current outlook; business leverage is at historically low levels and tends to be most influenced by demand, which is expected to grow only moderately in the period ahead based on the forecasts presented in the February Statement on Monetary Policy.","score":-0.4766576055745744,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":49,"end":55,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":143,"end":158,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"and that financial institutions continue to enhance their resilience.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"enhance","start":44,"end":51,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening crisis readiness and cyber and operational resilience in the Australian financial system is a regulatory priority.","score":-0.22634795467253555,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":14,"end":20,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Advancing digitalisation of the financial system increases the prospect that cyber-attacks could have systemic implications.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Advancing","start":0,"end":9,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Council of Financial Regulators (CFR) agencies are actively working with government and industry towards strengthening resilience within firms and across their networks, with a particular focus on better understanding service provider concentration risks, testing crisis management and cyber defence plans, and developing back-up payments capabilities.","score":0.2499999999999999,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":109,"end":122,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"better","start":201,"end":207,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":268,"end":274,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening preparedness for the potential impacts of geopolitical risk is increasingly important.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Heightened international tensions create the potential for adverse effects on the economy and financial system, including from cyber threats and conflicts.","score":-0.7845273796582746,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":59,"end":66,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"threats","start":133,"end":140,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"conflicts","start":145,"end":154,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The CFR agreed a work program in December 2024 to reinforce system-wide resilience to geopolitical risk.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The CFR noted that geopolitical risk is an increasing concern for regulators and industry internationally, and is likely fundamentally to characterise global affairs for some time.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"concern","start":54,"end":61,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]}]},{"date":"2025-10","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2025-10 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2025/oct/","sourcePdfPages":[4,7],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Original heading ellipses retained; standalone punctuation and endnotes excluded from counts.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"Global financial stability risks are elevated and an international shock could spill over to the Australian financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"In an environment of heightened risk in the international system, stress events have the potential to interact with – and amplify – existing vulnerabilities and generate disruptive shocks.","score":-0.6907747429922022,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":66,"end":72,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":141,"end":156,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptive","start":170,"end":180,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While April’s sharp global spike in market volatility did not give rise to significant financial stability concerns in Australia, this was likely helped by its short duration.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":43,"end":53,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"concerns","start":107,"end":115,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Volatility in international financial markets has subsided over recent months – to long-run average levels or below – as the prospect of the most severe form of retaliatory global trade war receded somewhat.","score":-0.8885233166386385,"words":[{"text":"Volatility","start":0,"end":10,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":146,"end":152,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"retaliatory","start":161,"end":172,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"war","start":186,"end":189,"base":-2.9,"score":-2.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, the international outlook remains clouded in uncertainty, including in relation to fiscal sustainability concerns in some advanced economies and the possible lagged effects of tariff increases on prices and activity in the United States.","score":-0.7227247225603619,"words":[{"text":"concerns","start":114,"end":122,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"lagged","start":167,"end":173,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The risk of regulatory fragmentation across the international financial system has also increased, as jurisdictions pursue diverging priorities, including in banking and digital assets regulation.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"These uncertainties add to the growing risks to the financial system stemming from cyber and operational incidents.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":105,"end":114,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Physical and transition risks associated with climate change, including rising uninsurability, also remain of concern.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"concern","start":110,"end":117,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"With such a wide range of risks, the possibility of a material shock to the international financial system is rising.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Three key vulnerabilities in the global financial system stand out as having the potential to significantly affect financial stability in Australia: • Vulnerabilities in key international financial markets, including sovereign debt markets, interacting with persistent vulnerabilities in the global non-bank financial institution (NBFI) sector.","score":-0.6485566468843295,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":10,"end":25,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"Vulnerabilities","start":151,"end":166,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"persistent","start":258,"end":268,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":269,"end":284,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Despite rising geopolitical tension, and fiscal sustainability challenges in a number of large advanced economies, risk premiums in global equity and credit markets remain low, and sovereign bond term premia are only around long-term averages.","score":0,"words":[{"text":"Despite","start":0,"end":7,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenges","start":63,"end":73,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Forward-looking measures of financial market volatility are also generally subdued.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":45,"end":55,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A reassessment of the likelihood or consequences of key risks materialising would therefore make international financial markets vulnerable to sharp corrections.","score":-0.709560194985489,"words":[{"text":"reassessment","start":2,"end":14,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerable","start":129,"end":139,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"corrections","start":149,"end":160,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Highly leveraged trading strategies employed by hedge funds, liquidity mismatches among bond funds, concentration in equity markets, and interlinkages across the global financial system, have the potential to amplify an adverse shock.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"mismatches","start":71,"end":81,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":220,"end":227,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Operational vulnerabilities resulting from increasing digitalisation and interconnectedness, with increased potential for operational incidents to interact with other stress events.","score":-0.7095601949854891,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":14,"end":29,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"incidents","start":136,"end":145,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":169,"end":175,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The rapid digital transformation of the financial system has the capacity to support efficiency and innovation.","score":0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"efficiency","start":85,"end":95,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"innovation","start":100,"end":110,"base":1.6,"score":1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"At the same time, it has heightened operational risks in an environment of rising cyber risk alongside elevated geopolitical tensions.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The system has also become more interconnected, in part reflecting third-party concentration risk in critical services (including where these are provided offshore).","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":101,"end":109,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The increased potential for operational incidents to occur alongside financial stress, such as a liquidity shock, increases the scope of coordination required across regulators, government and industries in responding to such events.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":40,"end":49,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":79,"end":85,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Longstanding vulnerabilities in China’s financial and property sectors.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":15,"end":30,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Persistent weakness in the property sector, amid a structural rebalancing in the Chinese economy, remains a vulnerability for real estate companies, local government finances and the wider Chinese financial system.","score":-0.7351470441147052,"words":[{"text":"Persistent","start":0,"end":10,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"weakness","start":11,"end":19,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerability","start":108,"end":121,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Chinese banks continue to experience pressure on margins and asset quality issues; liquidity concerns also emerged recently in pockets of the system.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"concerns","start":93,"end":101,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Authorities have intervened to recapitalise state-owned banks and encourage regional bank consolidation, and the ongoing local government debt swap program is helping to mitigate some of the pressures in the financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, these challenges are unlikely to dissipate for some time.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":15,"end":25,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"A material disruption to financial stability in China would affect the Australian financial system indirectly, via global risk sentiment and trade channels.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"disruption","start":11,"end":21,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If risks were to materialise, these vulnerabilities could spill over to the Australian financial system through three channels: • Via global financial markets.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":36,"end":51,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A significant increase in risk aversion in global markets could sharply increase financing costs, including in Australia, and restrict Australian firms’ and financial institutions’ access to funding and liquidity in global markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"sharply","start":64,"end":71,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A resulting tightening in financial conditions would intensify financial pressures on domestic borrowers and, if severe enough to strain financial institutions’ balance sheets, could limit credit availability in the Australian economy.","score":-0.6485566468843295,"words":[{"text":"tightening","start":12,"end":22,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":113,"end":119,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"strain","start":130,"end":136,"base":-0.2,"score":-0.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"It could also create liquidity strains for Australian banks and NBFIs, such as superannuation funds – although there is considerable scope for most borrowers and lenders to draw down on buffers in the event of a liquidity shock.","score":-0.295958174200194,"words":[{"text":"strains","start":31,"end":38,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Any depreciation of the exchange rate would also play a shock-absorbing role.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• Via the digital and physical infrastructure underpinning the Australian financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A direct and rapid impact on the Australian economy could arise from severe operational disruptions to key financial institutions, or to the financial or wider national infrastructure on which the financial system depends.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":69,"end":75,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptions","start":88,"end":99,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The potential effects on public confidence from disruptions of this nature would be highly dependent on the surrounding context.","score":-0.33995005182504245,"words":[{"text":"disruptions","start":48,"end":59,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening operational resilience in the Australian financial system has become a key regulatory priority.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Via the real economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A global economic downturn, particularly one that leads to a sharp slowdown in China (Australia’s most significant trading partner), could negatively affect Australia through trade channels – including commodity prices and investment – and spill over into weaker spending by Australian consumers and businesses.","score":-0.8555415792658776,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":18,"end":26,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"slowdown","start":67,"end":75,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"negatively","start":139,"end":149,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"weaker","start":256,"end":262,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As discussed below, the Australian financial system is well placed to continue supporting the economy in an economic downturn.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":117,"end":125,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system remains financially resilient overall.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The Australian financial system remains well placed to continue to provide vital services in the event of an economic downturn.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":118,"end":126,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If a significant economic downturn occurs, banks are well positioned to absorb large loan losses while continuing to support the economy through lending to households and businesses.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":26,"end":34,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":90,"end":96,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Australian banks’ resilience has been supported by a long period of prudent lending standards, the high quality and quantity of capital, and significant holdings of liquid assets.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Some borrowers continue to experience severe financial stress, but the overall share of such borrowers has remained small and losses to banks have been well contained by strong collateral values.","score":-0.2022886949696695,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":38,"end":44,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"stress","start":55,"end":61,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"losses","start":126,"end":132,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"strong","start":170,"end":176,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Non-performing loans remain small relative to banks’ capacity to absorb losses.","score":-0.401923825269382,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":72,"end":78,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The superannuation sector has tended to support financial stability in previous periods of financial stress, but building resilience to severe liquidity and operational shocks and managing a large and expanding market presence remain priorities.","score":-0.6485566468843295,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":101,"end":107,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"severe","start":136,"end":142,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Superannuation funds now account for around 160 per cent of Australian GDP and in aggregate the value of assets held by superannuation funds are expected to continue to grow until at least 2050.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The interconnections between superannuation funds and banks have the potential to transmit stress in a severe market-wide liquidity stress event.","score":-0.8019956080183264,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":91,"end":97,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":103,"end":109,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":132,"end":138,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As the sector’s foreign asset holdings continue to build, there will be a growing need to hedge foreign exchange risk, which will require careful liquidity management.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The April cyber-attacks on the sector have also highlighted the potential consequences of operational disruptions coinciding with stressed conditions in financial markets.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"disruptions","start":102,"end":113,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"stressed","start":130,"end":138,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In the months ahead, results from the first Australian Prudential Regulation Authority (APRA) system risk stress test will provide further insights into the interconnections between Australian banks and superannuation funds, and the interaction between financial and operational risks.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":106,"end":112,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Continued strengthening of superannuation funds’ governance, liquidity and operational risk management practices remain areas of ongoing focus for regulators.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":10,"end":23,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"General insurance firms remain well capitalised and profitable, but insurance affordability and availability may become increasingly challenging over time.","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"profitable","start":52,"end":62,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"challenging","start":133,"end":144,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Recent profitability in the sector has been supported by low claims, higher premiums and a moderation in the growth of reinsurance costs.","score":-0.1027330993875028,"words":[{"text":"profitability","start":7,"end":20,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"claims","start":61,"end":67,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Home insurance premiums remain at historically high levels, partly reflecting the increase in climate and weather events and related growth in reinsurance costs, and building cost inflation.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"These trends could continue as climate change intensifies weather-related risks to physical infrastructure over time.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Decreasing affordability of insurance could increase underinsurance, lowering the credit quality of existing mortgage loans.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The strong financial positions of most Australian households and businesses would limit the risk of widespread financial stress in an economic downturn.","score":-0.25000000000000006,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":4,"end":10,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":121,"end":127,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"downturn","start":143,"end":151,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Cash flow pressures have eased a little over the past year as inflation and interest rates have declined.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"declined","start":96,"end":104,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Supported by ongoing strength in the labour market and a recent increase in real household disposable income, the share of mortgagors in severe financial stress – where mortgage payments and spending on essentials exceeds their income – remains small and has declined further.","score":-0.5718850320700721,"words":[{"text":"strength","start":21,"end":29,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":137,"end":143,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":154,"end":160,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"declined","start":259,"end":267,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Most mortgagors have maintained large liquidity and equity buffers, which help insulate them and protect the banking system from loan losses in most plausible adverse circumstances.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":134,"end":140,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":159,"end":166,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While the share of companies entering insolvency remains elevated in the retail, hospitality and construction industries – where the operating environment has been challenging, particularly for smaller firms – at an economy-wide level the insolvency rate is around its longer run average.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"insolvency","start":38,"end":48,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenging","start":164,"end":175,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"insolvency","start":239,"end":249,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Further, generally high cash buffers and stable leverage ratios mean firms can manage fluctuations in their cash flows more readily than otherwise, reducing the likelihood of not meeting loan obligations.","score":0.295958174200194,"words":[{"text":"stable","start":41,"end":47,"base":1.2,"score":1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The forecasts presented in the August Statement on Monetary Policy (based on the market-implied cash rate path at that time) suggest that most households and businesses would see some improvements in their cash flow positions over the next year or so, supported by an improvement in the economic environment and easing financial conditions.","score":0.42146361521176223,"words":[{"text":"improvements","start":184,"end":196,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"improvement","start":268,"end":279,"base":2,"score":2,"notes":[]},{"text":"easing","start":312,"end":318,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, the most vulnerable borrowers will continue to face significant challenges and the outlook remains uncertain.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":18,"end":28,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"challenges","start":73,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"However, it is important that lending standards remain sound …","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"If heightened competition for business loans was associated with a material erosion in lending standards, it could lead to a build-up of vulnerabilities in the business sector and undermine future resilience.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"erosion","start":76,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":137,"end":152,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"undermine","start":180,"end":189,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Heightened competition for business loans, including from non-bank lenders, has supported credit availability for some businesses and reduced refinancing risks over the past year.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"To date, other than a slight easing in commercial real estate (CRE) lending standards, there has been little evidence of a broad decline in lending standards.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":29,"end":35,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"decline","start":129,"end":136,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"NBFI lenders, including private credit funds managing investor capital, are also emerging as an important source of financing for entities that cannot readily obtain bank financing, though these lenders are still not sufficiently large to be of systemic importance.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Nevertheless, with strong growth in business credit and information on some non-bank lending more limited than for banks, the RBA and other Council of Financial Regulators (CFR) agencies are monitoring conditions in this segment of the market.","score":0.6651548698133041,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":19,"end":25,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Macroprudential policy can play an important role in helping to contain housing-related vulnerabilities that could build over the monetary policy easing phase.","score":-0.4766576055745744,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":88,"end":103,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"easing","start":146,"end":152,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While lending standards have remained prudent, a material increase in riskier forms of lending in response to lower interest rates could contribute to a build-up in vulnerabilities.","score":-0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"riskier","start":70,"end":77,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":165,"end":180,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could occur in two distinct but related ways: by amplifying the housing price and credit cycle; and by increasing the risk that borrowers may struggle to service their loans in future.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"In turn, these can increase the risk, severity and macroeconomic implications of future shocks.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"severity","start":38,"end":46,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In the context of declining interest rates, the RBA supports APRA’s recent position to keep macroprudential settings steady, given any loosening has the potential to amplify macro-financial vulnerabilities.","score":-0.4766576055745744,"words":[{"text":"declining","start":18,"end":27,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":190,"end":205,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The RBA also supports efforts by APRA to work with industry to ensure a range of macroprudential tools could be deployed in a timely manner if needed.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"and that financial institutions in Australia continue to enhance their resilience to geopolitical and operational risk.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"enhance","start":57,"end":64,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Strengthening resilience to geopolitical and operational risk, including crisis recovery arrangements, is an increasing priority for regulators and industry.","score":-0.22634795467253555,"words":[{"text":"Strengthening","start":0,"end":13,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":73,"end":79,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Geopolitical risk is impinging on the global economy and financial system in complex ways and is introducing novel financial stability challenges.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":135,"end":145,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This includes a greater risk of concurrent financial and operational stress events, which complicates the nature of crisis response and the scope of coordination required across regulators, government and industries.","score":-0.7845273796582746,"words":[{"text":"greater","start":16,"end":23,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":69,"end":75,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"complicates","start":90,"end":101,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":116,"end":122,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Advancing digitalisation of the financial system also increases the prospect that cyber-attacks and operational incidents could have systemic implications.","score":0,"words":[{"text":"Advancing","start":0,"end":9,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"incidents","start":112,"end":121,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In response, CFR agencies are actively working with industry to strengthen the resilience of individual institutions and the wider system.","score":0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":64,"end":74,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This work includes: wide-ranging scenario analysis; testing of crisis management plans, with a focus on coordination across regulators, government and industry stakeholders; strengthening of cyber defences; identifying and managing service provider concentration risks; and developing capabilities to ensure Australians can continue to make and receive payments in the event of a material disruption to the payments system.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"crisis","start":63,"end":69,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"strengthening","start":174,"end":187,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruption","start":389,"end":399,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]}]},{"date":"2026-03","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2026-03 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2026/mar/","sourcePdfPages":[4,8],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Page breaks and bullet glyphs normalised; publication metadata excluded.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"Global financial stability risks are elevated in a rapidly evolving international environment.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Escalation of conflict in the Middle East has sparked a sharp increase in volatility in global financial markets, following a period of benign market conditions.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":14,"end":22,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"volatility","start":74,"end":84,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Volatility has been particularly pronounced in energy and other commodity markets, and this has spilled over to other asset classes.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Volatility","start":0,"end":10,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Just prior to the escalation of the conflict, some firms had experienced sharp equity price declines as investors reassessed the prospects for AI to affect business models.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":36,"end":44,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"declines","start":92,"end":100,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Conditions for private credit markets had also become more challenging.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":59,"end":70,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Yet risk premia in global equity and credit markets have remained fairly low by historical standards.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Additionally, even after an increase in government bond yields in several advanced economies, measures of sovereign bond term premia were well within historical ranges and markets remained functional.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Resilience in the global financial system prior to the escalation of the conflict had been supported by systemically important banks remaining profitable and well capitalised, and growth in the global economy over the past year exceeding most analyst expectations, with trade flows adjusting relatively quickly to changes in tariffs.","score":0.15309310892394862,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":73,"end":81,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"profitable","start":143,"end":153,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Corporate and household balance sheets in advanced economies have remained strong, although pockets of stress persist among non-prime borrowers.","score":-0.25000000000000006,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":75,"end":81,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":103,"end":109,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"persist","start":110,"end":117,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"At the time of writing, the conflict in the Middle East was evolving rapidly, with considerable uncertainty over its ultimate duration, scale and broader spillover effects.","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":28,"end":36,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Council of Financial Regulator (CFR) agencies are closely monitoring the situation and engaging with industry as appropriate.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Following a lengthy period where risk premia in global financial markets were unusually low, the escalation of conflict in the Middle East has sparked a substantial increase in volatility.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":111,"end":119,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"volatility","start":177,"end":187,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Given leverage and concentration risk in key global asset markets had increased materially in recent years, amid a period of low risk premia, this increases the potential for a disorderly repricing of assets in response to further adverse developments.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"disorderly","start":177,"end":187,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":231,"end":238,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The risk of significant operational, cyber and security incidents, which have risen over recent years, is also heightened at present.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":56,"end":65,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system has established a good degree of resilience to navigate through a high-risk international environment, though any of these events, if severe enough, could pose financial stability challenges in Australia.","score":-0.29595817420019405,"words":[{"text":"good","start":50,"end":54,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":166,"end":172,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenges","start":212,"end":222,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In this environment, the CFR agencies, including the RBA, continue to engage closely with industry to ensure that: • Australian financial institutions continue to build resilience and readiness to respond to extreme-but-plausible liquidity, operational and geopolitical risks that could interact • lending standards remain prudent amid an upswing in credit growth, with regulatory settings continuing to limit the build-up of systemic risk and thus foster confidence in the financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Global financial stability risks remain elevated due to heightened geopolitical tensions and several sources of uncertainty.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The global economic and financial outlook could deteriorate abruptly for any number of reasons, such as: • Potential for elevated geopolitical tensions to spillover into a severe international shock.","score":-0.7649686210234002,"words":[{"text":"deteriorate","start":48,"end":59,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"abruptly","start":60,"end":68,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severe","start":172,"end":178,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The geopolitical environment remains highly fluid.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The conflict in the Middle East could trigger a larger shock that destabilises the global economy, particularly if supply disruptions to oil and other commodity markets are prolonged.","score":-0.7590087349170742,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":4,"end":12,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptions","start":122,"end":133,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.715,"notes":[{"t":"boost","w":"particularly","m":1.225}],"note":"intensified particularly ×1.23"},{"text":"prolonged","start":173,"end":182,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Tensions among major global powers also have the potential to escalate, hostile cyber and other actions are intensifying, and strains in the international rules-based order are increasing alongside the risk of global geoeconomic fragmentation.","score":-0.7430377554349386,"words":[{"text":"escalate","start":62,"end":70,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"hostile","start":72,"end":79,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"strains","start":126,"end":133,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Potential for a sharp revision of the outlook for AI-related investments.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Should expectations around the productivity benefits of the surge in AI-related investment be reduced, it could lead to a significant downgrade in profitability forecasts and asset valuations.","score":-0.1027330993875028,"words":[{"text":"downgrade","start":134,"end":143,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"profitability","start":147,"end":160,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Negative consequences for asset quality in the financial system and investment plans in the real economy could result.","score":-0.5718850320700721,"words":[{"text":"Negative","start":0,"end":8,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Potential for confidence in institutional arrangements to be undermined or regulatory divergence.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"undermined","start":63,"end":73,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A sustained pattern of unconventional policy actions and a push for regulators to rebalance regulatory frameworks in favour of growth (relative to stability) could also undermine established institutional arrangements in some jurisdictions or lead to material regulatory divergence globally.","score":0.07722832842765422,"words":[{"text":"stability","start":147,"end":156,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"undermine","start":169,"end":178,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could affect policy credibility, give rise to a new wave of regulatory arbitrage and present additional challenges for multilateral cooperation.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":109,"end":119,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Potential for a disruptive crystallisation of macro-financial vulnerabilities in China.","score":-0.44043357076016854,"words":[{"text":"disruptive","start":18,"end":28,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":64,"end":79,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Chinese authorities have identified strong supply growth in the industrial sector and weak domestic demand continue to characterise the Chinese growth model, which, combined with low inflation relative to trading partners, has led to a real exchange rate depreciation.","score":0.1027330993875028,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":36,"end":42,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"weak","start":86,"end":90,"base":-1.9,"score":-1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This has contributed to strong exports and a rising current account surplus, which has amplified tensions with some trade partners.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":24,"end":30,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Longstanding vulnerabilities in parts of the Chinese financial system – including banks, non-banks and local governments – have been exacerbated by the ongoing weakness in the Chinese real estate sector.","score":-0.7095601949854891,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":13,"end":28,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"exacerbated","start":133,"end":144,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"weakness","start":160,"end":168,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Debt in the corporate sector continues to rise sharply as a share of GDP.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"sharply","start":47,"end":54,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Consolidated public debt in China is also expected to rise further, particularly given the need to support highly indebted local governments.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A disruptive crystallisation of these vulnerabilities could sharply increase risk aversion in global financial markets and result in reduced Chinese demand for Australian goods and services.","score":-0.6597279468503828,"words":[{"text":"disruptive","start":2,"end":12,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":38,"end":53,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"sharply","start":60,"end":67,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This environment increases the prospect for international developments to interact with existing vulnerabilities and affect financial stability in Australia.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":97,"end":112,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A disruptive adjustment in international financial markets could pose financial stability challenges in Australia.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"disruptive","start":2,"end":12,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenges","start":90,"end":100,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Two global vulnerabilities stand out in this regard: • Vulnerabilities arising from a large and growing stock of sovereign debt in key advanced economies, which could be subjected to a disorderly repricing if debt sustainability concerns were to escalate.","score":-0.8806721767742777,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":11,"end":26,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"Vulnerabilities","start":55,"end":70,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"subjected","start":170,"end":179,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disorderly","start":185,"end":195,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"concerns","start":229,"end":237,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"escalate","start":246,"end":254,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A sharp sell-off could be amplified by the growing role in these markets of leveraged, price-sensitive non-bank financial institutions (NBFIs).","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Projected fiscal deficits in a range of advanced economies are expected to add further to the supply of sovereign debt in the years ahead, at a time where price-sensitive NBFIs are becoming a key source of marginal demand.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"deficits","start":17,"end":25,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The historical experience suggests a sharp repricing or period of disorderly functioning in key international sovereign debt markets could spill over to Australian bond markets, presenting challenges to financial stability (discussed in more detail below).","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"disorderly","start":66,"end":76,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenges","start":189,"end":199,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Vulnerabilities arising from low risk premia and concentrated exposures in key international equity and credit markets, with the potential for disorderly price adjustments amplified by leverage and liquidity mismatches in global NBFIs.","score":-0.6808225270250103,"words":[{"text":"Vulnerabilities","start":2,"end":17,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disorderly","start":145,"end":155,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"mismatches","start":210,"end":220,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Global equity and bond markets remain vulnerable to sharp corrections.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":38,"end":48,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"corrections","start":58,"end":69,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Ongoing significant increases in exposure to the AI investment cycle across the financial system could also become a source of instability if the optimistic outlook for that sector is substantially revised.","score":-0.05157106231293966,"words":[{"text":"instability","start":127,"end":138,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"optimistic","start":146,"end":156,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"More generally, a further escalation in geopolitical tensions, or adverse policy or macro-financial developments, could trigger a sustained increase in risk aversion across global financial markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":66,"end":73,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A disruptive adjustment in international markets could spill over to Australia by sharply increasing domestic financing costs, restricting Australian firms’ and financial institutions’ access to funding and liquidity in global markets, and leading to substantial losses in the value of financial assets, including those held by households.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"disruptive","start":2,"end":12,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"sharply","start":82,"end":89,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"leading","start":240,"end":247,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":263,"end":269,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If severe enough, it could also limit credit availability in Australia and have consequences for the real economy.","score":-0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":3,"end":9,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Australian companies, banks and superannuation funds have taken steps to mitigate their exposure to shocks in global financial markets in recent years, including by building significant liquidity buffers, and any depreciation of the exchange rate could also play a shock-absorbing role for the Australian economy.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"However, a more shock-prone international environment means it is important that Australian borrowers and lenders maintain resilience.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"prone","start":22,"end":27,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Growing operational complexity and interconnectedness across the financial system is increasing non-financial vulnerabilities.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":110,"end":125,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These heighten the potential for operational incidents to have systemic financial consequences, including if they interact with other stress events.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":45,"end":54,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":134,"end":140,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"For example, an outage at a major financial institution or a service provider used by many financial institutions could disrupt funding markets or stop households and businesses from accessing their money.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"outage","start":16,"end":22,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disrupt","start":120,"end":127,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Operational resilience in the payments system and across financial market infrastructures is particularly critical to ensuring the steady flow of funds throughout the economy.","score":-0.38689790790672945,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":106,"end":114,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.625,"notes":[{"t":"boost","w":"particularly","m":1.25}],"note":"intensified particularly ×1.25"}]},{"text":"Complexity and interconnectedness can also increase as new technology is introduced to the financial system and has to interact with existing technological infrastructure.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"When episodes of financial and operational stress occur simultaneously, it also complicates the nature of crisis response and the scope of coordination required across regulators, government and industries.","score":-0.8555415792658776,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":43,"end":49,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"complicates","start":80,"end":91,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":106,"end":112,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The Australian financial system has a good degree of resilience, though the risk of a more material adverse shock has increased over recent weeks.","score":0.1027330993875028,"words":[{"text":"good","start":38,"end":42,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":100,"end":107,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If a significant economic downturn occurs, Australian banks are well positioned to absorb significant loan losses while continuing to support the economy through lending to households and businesses.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"downturn","start":26,"end":34,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"losses","start":107,"end":113,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks are well capitalised and have maintained prudent lending standards and provisioning.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Non-bank lenders and private credit firms have increased the availability of credit for both housing and business borrowers, which could result in higher loan losses in the years ahead, but their relatively small size means the systemic impact of stress in this sector would be limited.","score":-0.6757003498408973,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":159,"end":165,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"stress","start":247,"end":253,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Some borrowers continue to experience severe financial stress, but overall loan losses have remained low by historical standards, reflecting strong collateralisation of loans and the relatively small increase in non-performing loans over recent years.","score":-0.2022886949696695,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":38,"end":44,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"stress","start":55,"end":61,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"losses","start":80,"end":86,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"strong","start":141,"end":147,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"Banks’ liquid assets provide a buffer against unanticipated liquidity shocks, but it is important that these can be converted quickly into cash when necessary.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"against","start":38,"end":45,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"unanticipated","start":46,"end":59,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"As a large and growing part of the overall financial system, it is important that the superannuation sector continues to strengthen its approach to risk management.","score":0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":121,"end":131,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The value of assets managed by the superannuation sector now represents around 160 per cent of GDP, one-third of Australian financial system assets, and is expected to continue growing solidly until at least 2050.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This growth, coupled with an increasing share of Australians drawing retirement income streams from the system, means that further strengthening of superannuation funds’ governance, liquidity and operational risk management practices will remain a key focus for regulators.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":131,"end":144,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The general insurance sector is not currently a source of financial stability concern, but insurance affordability and availability may become increasingly challenging over time.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"concern","start":78,"end":85,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"challenging","start":156,"end":167,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The general insurance sector has remained well capitalised and profitable.","score":0.44043357076016854,"words":[{"text":"profitable","start":63,"end":73,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Home insurance premiums remain at historically high levels and further increases could increase underinsurance, lowering the credit quality of housing loans over time.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The forthcoming Climate Vulnerability Assessment by the Australian Prudential Regulation Authority’s (APRA) will help quantify how general insurance affordability may be affected by climate change.","score":-0.22634795467253557,"words":[{"text":"Vulnerability","start":24,"end":37,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The strong financial positions of most Australian households and businesses means that they are unlikely to be a source of instability, though financing pressures will increase for some if inflation is higher for longer than currently forecast.","score":0.2022886949696694,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":4,"end":10,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"instability","start":123,"end":134,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Borrowers are generally well placed to weather cost pressures stemming from the conflict in the Middle East and higher interest payments, though the most vulnerable borrowers will face growing challenges.","score":-0.6907747429922022,"words":[{"text":"conflict","start":80,"end":88,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerable","start":154,"end":164,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenges","start":193,"end":203,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Loan arrears have remained at low levels, supported by strength in the labour and housing markets.","score":0.17785756046362947,"words":[{"text":"arrears","start":5,"end":12,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"strength","start":55,"end":63,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Company insolvencies have stabilised at around longer run averages at an economy-wide level, although the share of companies entering insolvency remains elevated in the hospitality and construction sectors – where the operating environment has been more challenging, particularly for smaller firms.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"insolvencies","start":8,"end":20,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"insolvency","start":134,"end":144,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenging","start":254,"end":265,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Fundamentals continued to improve across most commercial real estate (CRE) markets and there continues to be little evidence of financial stress among owners of Australian CRE.","score":0.025811286645983332,"words":[{"text":"improve","start":26,"end":33,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":138,"end":144,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In this environment, financial institutions need to maintain focus on their resilience.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"It is important that lending standards remain prudent, and that regulation continues to limit the build-up of systemic risk and foster confidence in the financial system, given the backdrop of strong credit growth.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":193,"end":199,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While overall lending standards have remained sound, there is early evidence that some forms of riskier lending have picked up.","score":-0.33995005182504245,"words":[{"text":"riskier","start":96,"end":103,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Household indebtedness has been a key vulnerability domestically for many years, and high debt-to-income (DTI) lending to investors has increased of late, though remains below APRA’s recently implemented limit on high DTI lending.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"vulnerability","start":38,"end":51,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"late","start":149,"end":153,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"High loan-to-valuation ratio lending to first home buyers has also increased alongside the expansion of the Australian Government 5% Deposit Scheme in October 2025.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Heightened competition for business loans over recent years as bank and non-bank lenders seek to expand their business lending has supported credit availability for some businesses and reduced refinancing risks.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Relatedly, there has been a slight easing in CRE lending standards over the past year and incremental increases in risk appetite to lend to other businesses more recently.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"easing","start":35,"end":41,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, there has been little evidence of a broader decline in business lending standards to date.","score":-0.5023310149673528,"words":[{"text":"decline","start":53,"end":60,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The RBA and other CFR agencies continue to monitor lending conditions closely and are working together to deliver better regulation of the financial sector without compromising financial stability, consumer protection and market integrity.","score":0.6704783996548059,"words":[{"text":"better","start":114,"end":120,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"integrity","start":229,"end":238,"base":1.6,"score":1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In addition, the International Monetary Fund will be conducting an assessment during 2026 of the resilience of the financial sector, the quality of the regulatory and supervisory framework, and the capacity to manage and resolve financial crises as part of its Financial Sector Assessment Program.","score":0.02581128664598339,"words":[{"text":"resolve","start":221,"end":228,"base":1.6,"score":1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"crises","start":239,"end":245,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Enhancing resilience to operational and geopolitical risk is a regulatory priority around the world, including in Australia.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Enhancing","start":0,"end":9,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Recent operational incidents in Australia and abroad have highlighted the prospect for serious spillover effects to occur across a financial system that has become more complex and interconnected over time.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"incidents","start":19,"end":28,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"serious","start":87,"end":94,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These vulnerabilities are increasingly targeted by sophisticated malicious actors, including state-sponsored attackers, who can seek to co-opt or collaborate with insiders that have greater access to systems.","score":0.2022886949696694,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":6,"end":21,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"sophisticated","start":51,"end":64,"base":2.6,"score":2.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"malicious","start":65,"end":74,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"attackers","start":109,"end":118,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"collaborate","start":146,"end":157,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"greater","start":182,"end":189,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In Australia, CFR agencies are actively working with industry to strengthen the operational and cyber resilience of individual financial institutions, and the system as a whole.","score":0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":65,"end":75,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The RBA is also strengthening its operating model to support the critical payments settlements system more effectively, including by enhancing its cybersecurity controls and modernising core technology infrastructure.","score":0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":16,"end":29,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"critical","start":65,"end":73,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"enhancing","start":133,"end":142,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"To support the Payments System Board’s strategic objective to strengthen the resilience of the payments system, the RBA is actively pursuing a program of work aimed at identifying system-wide vulnerabilities in Australia’s payments system.","score":0.17785756046362944,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":62,"end":72,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":192,"end":207,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Over the course of 2025, CFR agencies have also worked with large financial institutions on scenario analysis to ensure there is a clear understanding of the potential impact of adverse geopolitical events, and on the development of robust payment system contingency capabilities.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":178,"end":185,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As part of this work, CFR agencies are strengthening their engagement with relevant government agencies and industry to support better information sharing and response coordination for extreme-but-plausible events.","score":0.4678158954296706,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":39,"end":52,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"better","start":128,"end":134,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]}]},{"date":"2026-10","title":"RBA Financial Stability Review · 2026-10 (Assessment)","source":"https://www.rba.gov.au/publications/fsr/2026/oct/","sourcePdfPages":[4,12],"scope":"Financial Stability Assessment narrative including substantive headings","quality":"Substantive headings included; interrupted sentence restored. Closing Box, Figure 1 and Endnotes excluded.","model":{"version":"1.0.0","profile":"finance"},"sentences":[{"text":"The Australian financial system has a good degree of resilience.","score":0.44043357076016854,"words":[{"text":"good","start":38,"end":42,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Most borrowers – households and businesses – are well positioned to manage through a period of slowing economic growth and declining housing prices.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"slowing","start":95,"end":102,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"declining","start":123,"end":132,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Bank lending standards have remained prudent in recent years and Australian banks have established large capital buffers to absorb significant loan losses if they were to arise under extreme-but-plausible scenarios.","score":-0.21436709567779696,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":148,"end":154,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"}]},{"text":"Non-bank lending activity in Australia has supported favourable business credit conditions over recent years; as the rapid growth in the domestic private credit market has been from a low base, concerns over credit quality in segments of this market raise more issues for investor protection than financial stability.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"concerns","start":194,"end":202,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In sum, while cyclical domestic risks continue to be closely monitored, they are not assessed as posing systemic financial stability risks at present.","score":0.1901172751573434,"words":[{"text":"posing","start":97,"end":103,"base":-1.5,"score":0.75,"notes":[{"t":"neg","w":"not"}],"note":"negated not"}]},{"text":"At the same time, threats to global financial stability continue to mount.","score":-0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"threats","start":18,"end":25,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Geopolitical risks are underscored by two ongoing conflicts and, over the medium term, intensifying strategic competition among major powers.","score":-0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"conflicts","start":50,"end":59,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Sovereign debt in some large overseas economies continues to trend higher from already elevated levels, and the increased participation in these markets by leveraged and price-sensitive investors is being closely monitored.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Low risk premia in major equity and credit markets have contributed to buoyant financial conditions for businesses, but these risk premia could move sharply higher in response to an adverse shock potentially in a disorderly manner.","score":-0.8673649643743679,"words":[{"text":"sharply","start":149,"end":156,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"adverse","start":182,"end":189,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"disorderly","start":213,"end":223,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"One possible trigger could be a shift in sentiment towards the AI investment boom, which is increasingly fuelled by expectations of sustained rapid earnings growth and a debt-financing cycle that is becoming more opaque and circular.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"boom","start":77,"end":81,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Alongside growing operational vulnerabilities – which are attracting significant attention from regulators domestically and abroad – these external factors are the most prominent threats to financial stability in Australia.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":30,"end":45,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"threats","start":179,"end":186,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The international economy and financial system have weathered several shocks in recent years with limited lasting disruption, but threats to international financial stability continue to mount.","score":-0.6651548698133041,"words":[{"text":"disruption","start":114,"end":124,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"threats","start":130,"end":137,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The apparent resilience in the international financial system over recent years has been encouraging, but longer-term threats to financial stability are intensifying, including from those originating outside the system.","score":-0.36115755925730764,"words":[{"text":"encouraging","start":89,"end":100,"base":2.4,"score":2.4,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"threats","start":118,"end":125,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"The global macro-financial environment is vulnerable to external shocks.","score":-0.22634795467253557,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":42,"end":52,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Conflicts in the Middle East and, to a lesser extent, Ukraine continue to disrupt global supply chains, and near-term resolutions to both conflicts are difficult to foresee.","score":-0.8481222655051033,"words":[{"text":"Conflicts","start":0,"end":9,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"disrupt","start":74,"end":81,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"conflicts","start":138,"end":147,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"difficult","start":152,"end":161,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"More broadly, geopolitical tensions beyond these regions appear likely to remain elevated for the foreseeable future.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The pace of technological development is also intensifying.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This brings with it not only opportunities for productivity-enhancing investment, but also the risk of significant operational disruptions, including from the complexity of integrating new technologies into existing systems and from the use of new technologies by malicious actors.","score":-0.7252671490103779,"words":[{"text":"opportunities","start":29,"end":42,"base":1.6,"score":-0.96,"notes":[{"t":"neg","w":"not"},{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"negated not · before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"enhancing","start":60,"end":69,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"disruptions","start":127,"end":138,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"malicious","start":264,"end":273,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"AI is a current example and quantum decryption will become an increasing threat if post-quantum cryptography is not implemented early enough.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"threat","start":73,"end":79,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Meanwhile, longer-term shifts in climate patterns, and an increased frequency and severity of extreme weather, also contribute to a more challenging environment.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"severity","start":82,"end":90,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"challenging","start":137,"end":148,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These external factors, combined with the increasing complexity and interconnectivity of the domestic and international financial systems, could be making a system-wide shock more likely and its potential consequences more severe.","score":-0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":223,"end":229,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In particular, the potential for financial and non-financial risks to interact in complex and novel ways is a growing focus of financial system regulators in Australia and internationally.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Overall, the Australian financial system is well positioned to absorb shocks but continued focus and action is required from industry to ensure this remains the case.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• In an environment of rapid technological change, increased concentration and connectedness, strengthening operational resilience requires ongoing action across the financial system.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":94,"end":107,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"There is a varying level of preparedness across the system and it is essential that operational resilience is given due focus including by critical service providers such as operators of financial market infrastructure.","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":139,"end":147,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• To maintain financial resilience, it is important that lending standards remain sound in the face of ongoing strong competition in lending.","score":0.5106070566382844,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":111,"end":117,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"It is also important that as the superannuation sector grows, its liquidity risk management practices continue to strengthen.","score":0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":114,"end":124,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• Overall risk management and recoverability planning must continue to evolve to meet new challenges.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenges","start":90,"end":100,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The growing risk that financial and non-financial risks crystallise together suggests industry needs to prepare for scenarios that are more multifaceted than in the past.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This includes the ability to respond and recover quickly from a wide range of eventualities.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Consideration of more extreme scenarios and stress tests, and more rigorous crisis simulations and other crisis preparedness activities – including at the industry-wide level and across regulators and government – are particularly pertinent in this environment.","score":-0.9000703207408192,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":44,"end":50,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":76,"end":82,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":105,"end":111,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"CFR agencies will continue to work with industry on preparedness initiatives.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"One area of related attention is the growing concentration of common service providers within and to the financial sector, which means that the effects of a major operational disruption at one service provider could cascade across a wide range of entities.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"disruption","start":175,"end":185,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The evolution in the geopolitical and cyber risk landscapes also raises the prospect that opportunistic cyber-attacks on key institutions occur at a moment of generalised financial stress, potentially interacting in a way that undermines confidence in the financial system and results in system-wide consequences that are complex to manage.","score":-0.6485566468843293,"words":[{"text":"opportunistic","start":90,"end":103,"base":-0.1,"score":-0.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":181,"end":187,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"undermines","start":227,"end":237,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Recent developments related to global sovereign bond markets, the AI investment boom, low market risk premia and operational resilience are also reinforcing risks to international financial system stability.","score":0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"boom","start":80,"end":84,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stability","start":197,"end":206,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Sovereign debt in some large overseas economies is trending higher from already elevated levels, and the recent increase in government bond yields will increase debt-servicing costs.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The rise in government bond yields over recent months has been driven by a number of factors, including a rise in expected policy rate paths as well as competition for debt funding from AI-related corporate bond issuance.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"While the absence of credible medium-term fiscal frameworks in several large advanced economies has yet to elicit a major focus from financial market participants, it is possible that debt sustainability concerns begin to mount if bond yields continue to rise without a commensurate increase in long-term growth expectations.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"absence","start":10,"end":17,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"concerns","start":204,"end":212,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Price-sensitive investors are once again accounting for a large share of activity in government bond markets (as central bank purchases have abated), and these participants are requiring higher compensation for risk.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Some, including hedge funds, are using significant amounts of leverage in these markets to profit from small arbitrage opportunities.","score":0.38181916792267817,"words":[{"text":"opportunities","start":119,"end":132,"base":1.6,"score":1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This raises the prospect of increased volatility – and potentially a disruptive sell-off – in core bond markets that are central to the operation of the global financial system.","score":-0.5858817654461621,"words":[{"text":"volatility","start":38,"end":48,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptive","start":69,"end":79,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While the upward move in sovereign bond yields has generally been orderly to date, a disorderly repricing in government bond markets could have adverse spillover effects into other asset classes.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"disorderly","start":85,"end":95,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":144,"end":151,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Elevated sovereign debt burdens also mean that the capacity of some governments to respond to economic shocks with fiscal support is more limited.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"burdens","start":24,"end":31,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The rapid pace of developments in AI are boosting expectations for earnings growth and productivity, but may also be increasing risks to financial stability via operational and financial risk channels.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Over the medium term, AI has the potential to support productivity growth and strengthen operational resilience by improving the cyber defence capabilities of banks, financial market infrastructures (FMIs) and other financial institutions.","score":0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"strengthen","start":78,"end":88,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"improving","start":115,"end":124,"base":1.8,"score":1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"But rapid advancements in AI could also worsen the cyber threat landscape before these defensive capabilities are fully developed, increasing the likelihood that underlying operational vulnerabilities could be exposed.","score":-0.9081213751352621,"words":[{"text":"advancements","start":10,"end":22,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"worsen","start":40,"end":46,"base":-2.3,"score":-2.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"threat","start":57,"end":63,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"defensive","start":87,"end":96,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":185,"end":200,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"},{"text":"exposed","start":210,"end":217,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"This includes how AI capabilities interact with the development of other emerging technologies, such as quantum computing.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The net impact of AI on cybersecurity will depend on a number of factors, including how quickly and thoroughly firms respond.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Even prior to the emergence of frontier AI models this year, the threat of system-wide operational disruptions had already been rising over recent years due to worsening geopolitical conflicts and the growing prevalence of common service providers – including from outside the financial sector (see 4.1 Focus Topic: Operational Risk and Financial Stability).","score":-0.885989313829565,"words":[{"text":"threat","start":65,"end":71,"base":-2.4,"score":-2.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptions","start":99,"end":110,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[]},{"text":"worsening","start":160,"end":169,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]},{"text":"conflicts","start":183,"end":192,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"The exposure of financial market participants to the AI investment boom is increasing at a time when risk premia in global equity and credit markets are low by historical standards.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"boom","start":67,"end":71,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Compressed risk premia in major markets have supported financing conditions for businesses, but also leave them vulnerable to sharp repricing if there were to be a sudden shift in global risk appetite.","score":-0.3291459381423852,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":112,"end":122,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"One trigger could be a shift in sentiment about the outlook for AI-related profitability of ‘hyperscalers’ (large AI-related companies) with the effect amplified by rising concentration and leverage in equity markets, particularly in the United States.","score":0.27321288529447424,"words":[{"text":"profitability","start":75,"end":88,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"To fund the surge in AI-related investment, a number of large firms have increasingly turned to both on and off-balance sheet leverage, which could create hidden and opaque interlinkages.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Some arrangements now involve circular financing models where hyperscalers provide vendor financing for key customers (see Box: Funding the AI investment boom).","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"boom","start":154,"end":158,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Over time, this could lead to a further build-up of vulnerabilities in the financial system.","score":-0.15309310892394865,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":52,"end":67,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Stability risks arising from activity in global private credit markets have also grown, although spillovers to the core banking system have been limited.","score":0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"Stability","start":0,"end":9,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Investor concerns over asset quality in several international private credit funds have remained elevated following the high-profile defaults of some private credit borrowers in late 2025 and early 2026.","score":-0.7579367289598671,"words":[{"text":"concerns","start":9,"end":17,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"defaults","start":133,"end":141,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"late","start":178,"end":182,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"For some private credit funds, the concerns centre around their exposure to sectors perceived as negatively affected by AI and some funds in the sector more broadly have had to enforce limits on investors redeeming their capital.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"concerns","start":35,"end":43,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"negatively","start":97,"end":107,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"These developments have drawn attention to vulnerabilities in segments of the private credit market, though stress has been largely idiosyncratic so far.","score":-0.5267415375673765,"words":[{"text":"vulnerabilities","start":43,"end":58,"base":-0.6,"score":-0.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":108,"end":114,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In major advanced economies, linkages between private credit funds and the core of the financial system are estimated by authorities to be growing from a low base, though limited data availability make it challenging for authorities to monitor the sector.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"challenging","start":205,"end":216,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"External shocks to the Australian financial system could transmit through various channels, depending on the context.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"In the event of a period of severe global market stress, financial conditions in Australia are likely to tighten.","score":-0.6597279468503829,"words":[{"text":"severe","start":28,"end":34,"base":-1.6,"score":-1.6,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":49,"end":55,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"A further escalation in geopolitical tensions or adverse policy or macro-financial developments – including in relation to sovereign debt sustainability or a deterioration in expectations for AI profitability – could trigger disorderly asset price corrections, particularly where investors have been using leverage to boost returns.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":49,"end":56,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"deterioration","start":158,"end":171,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"profitability","start":195,"end":208,"base":1.1,"score":1.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"disorderly","start":225,"end":235,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"corrections","start":248,"end":259,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"boost","start":318,"end":323,"base":1.7,"score":1.7,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This could impair market functioning and elicit a sustained increase in risk aversion across global financial markets.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"impair","start":11,"end":17,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Australia is unlikely to be immune should international funding conditions abruptly tighten, given that equity risk premia, corporate bond spreads and sovereign bond term premia in Australia tend to move closely with those in other advanced economies.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"abruptly","start":75,"end":83,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While large Australian corporations have taken steps over recent years to mitigate offshore refinancing risk and banks have similarly reduced their exposure to offshore funding risk, such a shock would likely affect access to financing by businesses and financial institutions.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"A major operational disruption – originating in Australia or overseas – has the potential to interact with traditional financial risks in complex ways.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"disruption","start":20,"end":30,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Depending on the circumstances, the event could undermine public confidence in the Australian financial system and could quickly translate to financial stress and economic disruption if payments were to stop flowing for a time and people temporarily lost access to their funds.","score":-0.8316320352807864,"words":[{"text":"undermine","start":48,"end":57,"base":-1.2,"score":-1.2,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":152,"end":158,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruption","start":172,"end":182,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"lost","start":250,"end":254,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If an outage occurs at a common service provider – such as an FMI or key technology provider – the effects could be widespread.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"outage","start":6,"end":12,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Unlike financial risks, whose emergence is likely to be largely confined to the regulated financial sector, operational risks to financial stability can originate from a much broader range of entities including common utility or technology providers, over which supervisors have limited oversight.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"confined","start":64,"end":72,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"If an operational outage impaired the provision of liquidity to the financial system, this could severely affect the crisis response (see 4.1 Focus Topic: Operational Risk and Financial Stability).","score":-0.9021745222664632,"words":[{"text":"outage","start":18,"end":24,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"impaired","start":25,"end":33,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"severely","start":97,"end":105,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":117,"end":123,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Furthermore, in the event that a major shock severely affected the economy of a key trading partner, the cash flows of Australian exporters would be affected with downstream consequences for employees, creditors and suppliers, although any depreciation of the exchange rate would provide some offsetting support to the economy over time.","score":-0.4588314677411235,"words":[{"text":"severely","start":45,"end":53,"base":-2,"score":-2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Domestic cyclical developments do not pose systemic risks to financial stability in Australia at present, but are being closely monitored.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Most Australian borrowers appear well positioned to manage ongoing cost pressures and declining housing prices.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"declining","start":86,"end":95,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Most households have substantial cash flow and saving buffers, which supports overall resilience in the system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Under adverse-but-plausible scenarios for housing price declines, most borrowers would still have equity buffers in their property given the preceding run-up in prices and prudent lending standards.","score":-0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"adverse","start":6,"end":13,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"declines","start":56,"end":64,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"This provides these borrowers the option of making the decision – though difficult and disruptive – to sell their property to pay off their loan fully if they experience acute financial stress.","score":-0.7649686210234002,"words":[{"text":"difficult","start":73,"end":82,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"disruptive","start":87,"end":97,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":186,"end":192,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Most businesses are also expected to remain resilient in the period ahead, although smaller businesses and those in more energy-intensive or cyclical industries with fewer buffers, such as transport, hospitality and construction, are more vulnerable to cost pressures.","score":-0.22634795467253557,"words":[{"text":"vulnerable","start":239,"end":249,"base":-0.9,"score":-0.9,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This is particularly the case if a significant weakening in aggregate demand were to make it harder for them to raise prices to cover their higher costs.","score":-0.318210996771242,"words":[{"text":"weakening","start":47,"end":56,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"In short, while there are pockets of stress in the household and business sectors, both sectors display a good level of resilience overall, as reflected in low loan arrears.","score":-0.3399500518250425,"words":[{"text":"stress","start":37,"end":43,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"good","start":106,"end":110,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"arrears","start":165,"end":172,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks are well positioned to weather a material deterioration in the housing market and a softening in economic conditions more broadly.","score":-0.3611575592573076,"words":[{"text":"deterioration","start":48,"end":61,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Banks have maintained prudent lending standards, and the high quantity and quality of capital in the banking system means banks could absorb losses from an adverse macroeconomic shock while continuing to extend credit.","score":-0.6369499429264264,"words":[{"text":"losses","start":141,"end":147,"base":-1.7,"score":-1.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":156,"end":163,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"As abroad, regulators in Australia have somewhat less visibility of risks from non-bank lender activity (including private credit), though the financial stability implications of this sector are currently limited by its relatively small size and limited interconnections with Australian banks.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Non-bank lenders have provided an alternative source of financing for businesses in Australia, accounting for around 10 per cent of total business debt outstanding.","score":0.6123724356957946,"words":[{"text":"outstanding","start":152,"end":163,"base":3,"score":3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Within this cohort of lenders, private credit funds have expanded over recent years but from a small base and, importantly, banks’ exposures to them remain limited.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Relative to the US private credit industry, Australian private credit funds are less exposed to companies disrupted by AI, though are more exposed to real estate, including construction and development.","score":-0.4766576055745744,"words":[{"text":"exposed","start":85,"end":92,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"disrupted","start":106,"end":115,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"exposed","start":139,"end":146,"base":-0.3,"score":-0.3,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"While investors in Australian private credit funds may face heightened risks of lower-than-expected (or negative) returns in a downturn, and this could affect the supply of new financing to real estate construction in particular, the implications for the broader stability of the financial system would be limited.","score":-0.5718850320700721,"words":[{"text":"negative","start":104,"end":112,"base":-2.7,"score":-2.7,"notes":[]},{"text":"downturn","start":127,"end":135,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"stability","start":263,"end":272,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"However, there are a number of investor-protection issues relating to opaque valuation practices and uneven standards of governance and risk management that the Australian Securities and Investments Commission is addressing (see Box: Private credit in Australia).","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"The Australian financial system is well placed to absorb financial shocks, but continued action is required to safeguard overall resilience.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Operational resilience requires ongoing action.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• Improving operational resilience has become a prominent focus of regulators, banks, FMIs, superannuation funds and other financial institutions.","score":0.4214636152117623,"words":[{"text":"Improving","start":2,"end":11,"base":1.8,"score":1.8,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"This includes the development of robust contingency and recovery plans and crisis response capabilities.","score":-0.6248933269389457,"words":[{"text":"crisis","start":75,"end":81,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"There is, however, a varying level of preparedness for operational disruptions across the financial system.","score":-0.4766576055745744,"words":[{"text":"disruptions","start":67,"end":78,"base":-1.4,"score":-1.4,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"• In an environment of rapid technological change, increased concentration, rising interconnectedness and heightened geopolitical risk, maintaining and building operational resilience will require ongoing vigilance across the financial system.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"This includes action by highly critical service providers such as FMIs.","score":-0.39993867171725506,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":31,"end":39,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.6900000000000002,"notes":[{"t":"boost","w":"highly","m":1.3}],"note":"intensified highly ×1.30"}]},{"text":"Austraclear is a particular focus for the RBA in its supervision activities at present given Austraclear’s central role in the financial system and the issues identified in relation to its operational resilience, including the ability to maintain critical services during a prolonged outage.","score":-0.7430377554349386,"words":[{"text":"critical","start":247,"end":255,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"prolonged","start":274,"end":283,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"outage","start":284,"end":290,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"Capital and liquidity resilience must be maintained.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"• It is important that lending standards remain sound in the face of continued strong competition in lending so that resilience is not eroded in an environment more prone to shocks.","score":0.37155736727027766,"words":[{"text":"strong","start":79,"end":85,"base":2.3,"score":2.3,"notes":[]},{"text":"eroded","start":135,"end":141,"base":-1.5,"score":0.75,"notes":[{"t":"neg","w":"not"}],"note":"negated not"},{"text":"prone","start":165,"end":170,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• The superannuation sector has been an important source of resilience and stability in the broader financial system over the years, but ongoing work to strengthen liquidity risk management practices is warranted as the sector continues to grow in size and as a larger share of superannuation balances become accessible to members.","score":0.5718850320700721,"words":[{"text":"stability","start":75,"end":84,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":0.5}],"note":"before contrast ×0.5"},{"text":"strengthen","start":153,"end":163,"base":1.3,"score":1.3,"notes":[{"t":"but","w":1.5}],"note":"after contrast ×1.5"}]},{"text":"A broader strengthening of risk management practices will be an important counterweight to growing financial stability risks, particularly external shocks.","score":0.49391458057363097,"words":[{"text":"strengthening","start":10,"end":23,"base":2.2,"score":2.2,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"• As structural and cyclical risks to financial stability are mounting, particularly from overseas, the financial sector must position itself to be able to maintain continuity of service across a range of adverse scenarios.","score":0,"words":[{"text":"able","start":148,"end":152,"base":1.5,"score":1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"adverse","start":205,"end":212,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]}]},{"text":"It is possible that financial and non-financial (particularly operational) risks crystalise in complex ways in the future.","score":0,"words":[]},{"text":"Robust and creative use of scenario analysis, stress testing, crisis simulations and other crisis-preparedness activities are particularly valuable in this environment.","score":-0.6678294453976725,"words":[{"text":"creative","start":11,"end":19,"base":1.9,"score":1.9,"notes":[]},{"text":"stress","start":46,"end":52,"base":-1.8,"score":-1.8,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":62,"end":68,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"crisis","start":91,"end":97,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"valuable","start":139,"end":147,"base":2.1,"score":2.625,"notes":[{"t":"boost","w":"particularly","m":1.25}],"note":"intensified particularly ×1.25"}]},{"text":"The member agencies of the Council of Financial Regulators are increasingly engaging with regulated entities, industry groups and government on a range of crisis scenarios, including scenarios that consider a broader range of possibilities than in the past, with particular focus on geopolitical shocks and multi-day operational outages at critical infrastructures.","score":-0.8359758677591161,"words":[{"text":"crisis","start":155,"end":161,"base":-3.1,"score":-3.1,"notes":[]},{"text":"outages","start":329,"end":336,"base":-1.5,"score":-1.5,"notes":[]},{"text":"critical","start":340,"end":348,"base":-1.3,"score":-1.3,"notes":[]}]}]}];
